::GEO_U04_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar figures have corresponding sides 5 and 20. What is the scale factor from first to second? {
=A. 4#20 divided by 5 equals 4.
~B. 15#This subtracts instead of comparing by ratio.
~C. 25#This adds the side lengths.
~D. &frac14;#This is the reverse scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A similar triangle has scale factor 3. A side of 11 becomes what length? {
~A. 14#Adding 3 does not use scale factor.
=B. 33#Multiply 11 by 3.
~C. 8#Subtracting 3 does not use scale factor.
~D. 3.67#Dividing by 3 scales in reverse.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A map scale is 1 inch to 25 miles. How far is 4 inches? {
~A. 29 miles#Adding 4 and 25 does not use the scale.
~B. 6.25 miles#Dividing 25 by 4 reverses the relationship.
=C. 100 miles#Four inches represents 4 times 25 miles.
~D. 75 miles#That would match 3 inches, not 4.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A 5-ft person casts a 6-ft shadow. A tree casts an 18-ft shadow. Assuming similar triangles, how tall is the tree? {
~A. 17 ft#Adding 12 to the height copies a difference rather than a ratio.
~B. 21.6 ft#Multiplying by 4.32 does not use the shadow scale factor.
~C. 10 ft#This does not match the scale factor of 3.
=D. 15 ft#The shadow scale factor is 18 divided by 6, or 3; 5 times 3 is 15.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two congruent figures are similar with what scale factor? {
=A. 1#Conguent figures have equal corresponding lengths, so the scale factor is 1.
~B. 0#A scale factor of 0 collapses lengths.
~C. 2#A scale factor of 2 changes size.
~D. &frac12;#A scale factor of &frac12; changes size.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model car is 12 cm long at a 1:20 scale. What is the actual length? {
~A. 32 cm#Adding 20 does not use the scale ratio.
=B. 240 cm#Actual length is 20 times the model length: 12 times 20.
~C. 8 cm#Subtracting 20 is not meaningful here.
~D. 0.6 cm#Dividing by 20 scales in the wrong direction.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A drawing uses scale factor &frac15;. An actual length is 60 cm. What is the drawing length? {
~A. 55 cm#Subtracting 5 does not use scale.
~B. 65 cm#Adding 5 does not use scale.
=C. 12 cm#60 times &frac15; equals 12.
~D. 300 cm#This uses the reciprocal scale and scales up.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>If corresponding sides are 7 to 14 and 9 to x, what is x? {
~A. 16#Adding 7 repeats a difference rather than a scale.
~B. 11#Adding 2 does not use the scale factor.
~C. 4.5#Dividing by 2 scales in the wrong direction.
=D. 18#The scale factor is 2, so 9 becomes 18.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which proportion matches corresponding sides 3 to 12 and 5 to x? {
=A. <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub> = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>#Corresponding sides must be placed in the same order in both ratios.
~B. <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>#This compares sides from different figures in the first ratio.
~C. 3 + 12 = 5 + x#Similarity uses multiplicative ratios, not addition.
~D. 3x = 5#This omits the corresponding side length 12.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A 2-inch drawing length represents 18 feet. How many feet does 5 inches represent? {
~A. 23 feet#Adding 5 to 18 does not use the scale.
=B. 45 feet#The scale is 9 feet per inch, so 5 inches represents 45 feet.
~C. 36 feet#This would match 4 inches at 9 feet per inch.
~D. 7.2 feet#Dividing 36 by 5 does not match the scale relationship.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A side of 16 corresponds to 10 in a smaller similar figure. What is the scale factor from larger to smaller? {
~A. 6#This subtracts side lengths.
~B. 26#This adds side lengths.
=C. 0.625#The scale factor is 10 divided by 16, or 0.625.
~D. 1.6#This is the reverse scale factor from smaller to larger.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A similar polygon has scale factor 2.5. A 4-unit side becomes what length? {
~A. 6.5 units#Adding 2.5 does not use scale factor.
~B. 1.6 units#Dividing by 2.5 scales in reverse.
~C. 8 units#This uses a scale factor of 2, not 2.5.
=D. 10 units#4 times 2.5 equals 10.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A 6-cm model represents 150 cm. What is the scale factor from model to actual? {
=A. 25#150 divided by 6 equals 25.
~B. 144#This subtracts lengths instead of finding the ratio.
~C. 156#This adds lengths instead of finding the ratio.
~D. 0.04#This is the reverse scale factor from actual to model.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A shadow problem creates similar triangles. A 4-ft pole casts an 8-ft shadow; a building casts a 40-ft shadow. What is the building height? {
~A. 36 ft#Subtracting or using the shadow difference does not preserve the ratio.
=B. 20 ft#The shadow scale factor is 5, so 4 times 5 is 20.
~C. 44 ft#Adding 40 and 4 does not use similar triangles.
~D. 10 ft#This uses only half the needed scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar pentagons have scale factor 0.5 from P to Q. A side in P is 18. What is the corresponding side in Q? {
~A. 18.5#Adding 0.5 does not use scale.
~B. 36#This uses the reciprocal and scales up.
=C. 9#18 times 0.5 equals 9.
~D. 17.5#Subtracting 0.5 does not use scale.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>What information is needed to set up a proportion for similar figures? {
~A. Only the area color#Color is unrelated to proportional side lengths.
~B. Only the number of vertices#The number of vertices does not determine side ratios.
~C. Only the page size#Page size does not provide corresponding lengths.
=D. Corresponding side lengths#Proportions compare matching corresponding sides.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A scale factor from A to B is 4. A side in B is 52. What was the corresponding side in A? {
=A. 13#Divide 52 by 4 to reverse the scale factor.
~B. 56#Adding 4 does not reverse scale.
~C. 48#Subtracting 4 does not reverse scale.
~D. 208#Multiplying by 4 scales from A to B again.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A similar rectangle has width 30 corresponding to original width 12. What is the scale factor? {
~A. 18#This subtracts widths.
=B. 2.5#30 divided by 12 equals 2.5.
~C. 42#This adds widths.
~D. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A blueprint has a 3-inch wall representing 24 feet. What does 7 inches represent? {
~A. 31 feet#Adding 7 and 24 does not use the scale.
~B. 72 feet#This would match 9 inches at the same scale.
=C. 56 feet#The scale is 8 feet per inch, so 7 inches represents 56 feet.
~D. 3.43 feet#Dividing 24 by 7 does not use the unit rate correctly.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>If x corresponds to 24 and 5 corresponds to 15, what is x? {
~A. 12#This uses an incorrect scale factor.
~B. 34#Adding the known values is not proportional reasoning.
~C. 72#This multiplies 24 by 3 instead of reversing the scale.
=D. 8#The scale factor is 3, so x times 3 equals 24; x is 8.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A scale drawing is 9 cm tall and the actual object is 36 cm tall. What is the scale factor from drawing to actual? {
=A. 4#36 divided by 9 equals 4.
~B. 27#This subtracts heights.
~C. 45#This adds heights.
~D. 0.25#This is the reverse scale factor from actual to drawing.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar triangles have scale factor &frac34; from large to small. A large side is 20. What is the small side? {
~A. 23#Adding 3 does not use the scale factor.
=B. 15#20 times &frac34; equals 15.
~C. 80#Multiplying by 4 does not use &frac34;.
~D. 26.67#Dividing by &frac34; scales in the opposite direction.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student sets 4 + 6 = 10 + x for similar figures. What is the misconception? {
~A. Using corresponding sides#Using corresponding sides is correct; the issue is the operation.
~B. Using too many variables#A variable may be used for an unknown side.
=C. Using addition instead of ratios#Similar figures are solved with proportional ratios, not additive sums.
~D. Checking scale factor#Checking scale factor is part of the correct method.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true for congruent two-dimensional figures? {
~A. Corresponding sides are always doubled.#Doubling describes a similar figure with scale factor 2, not congruence.
~B. Angles are unrelated.#Congruent figures have matching corresponding angles.
~C. Scale factor cannot be found.#The scale factor is 1 for congruent figures.
=D. Corresponding sides are equal.#Congruent figures have matching side lengths and angle measures.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A side length changes from 2.4 to 12 in a similar figure. What scale factor was used? {
=A. 5#12 divided by 2.4 equals 5.
~B. 9.6#This subtracts side lengths.
~C. 14.4#This adds side lengths.
~D. 0.2#This is the reverse scale factor.
}
